 @fredEVOIX  Please stop comparing 80 cards to 90 cards.  90 cards are the replacement or alternative to SLI, and it has always been that way.  The difference is that the GTX 490, 590, and 690 were still ran in SLI with two dies and mirrored VRAM on the same PCB whereas now we have access to all of the VRAM available with all those extra cores on a single die.  Just like SLI the 90 cards do not scale like you think 2x 5080's would scale in performance, but the 90 cards do scale better than cards ran in SLI configuration.

The claim is that the 5070 will play and feel like the 4090, a claim that isn't all that unreasonable.  Year over year the new 70 cards perform around the last gen 80 cards, DLSS improvements have been a boon to gaming, and these new generated frames can really go a long way to giving access to 4k 60fps full RT for the mid tier and it be playable at medium or high settings.

The thing is that nVidia always delivers on their marketing, even when what they report is underwhelming to gamers.  AMD always has a big hype that leads to a big let down.  We will see what happens in a few weeks.

